postgres.h and tupmacs.h: a Datum is __uintcap_t on Capstone (intcap plan, Phase D).

A Datum carries pointers, and on Capstone a pointer is a capability. As uintptr_t, an
8-byte integer, it lost the tag, and the first pointer read back from one faulted
(create pg_proc, GetTableAmRoutine+0x38). __uintcap_t is an integer stored as a
capability: pointers keep their tag, integers keep their value, and arithmetic runs on
the value.

SIZEOF_DATUM becomes the width of the value (8), not of the storage (16). Every
"#if SIZEOF_DATUM == 8" in the tree means the value: 64-bit sort abbreviations, hashes,
and byval int8/float8. tupmacs.h stores and fetches 8-byte byval attributes as the
8-byte integers they are on disk. It used to do that as `case sizeof(Datum)`, which is
16 here.

nodes.h declares outDatum and readDatum with uintptr_t, to stay free of postgres.h;
they take the same type as Datum now.

